"""
unit1_scripts_l06_l08.py
High-volume 20-25 minute broadcast tiki-taka scripts for Lectures 06, 07, and 08.
"""

SCRIPTS_L06 = {
    "1": "Prof. Park: Welcome everyone to Lecture 06 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me at the teaching desk is our graduate teaching assistant, Sora.\nTA Sora: Hello everyone! Today, we complete Section 1.2 on workbook pages 10 and 11: Negative Exponents, Power Rules, and Master Simplifications. Today is where all our exponent knowledge comes together into an unstoppable algebraic toolkit. Students are often frightened when they first see negative exponents—they mistakenly assume that a negative exponent turns the entire number negative. That is completely untrue! A negative exponent has nothing to do with whether a number is positive or negative. A negative exponent is simply an elevator pass: it tells a factor to take the elevator across the fraction bar to the opposite floor!\nProf. Park: That is such an intuitive analogy, Sora! Right here in Bozeman, our local optics and photonics industry—often called 'Photonics Valley'—develops ultra-precise lasers for scientific research, atmospheric monitoring, and aerospace sensors. Engineers measuring laser wavelengths routinely work with numbers like 5 times 10 to the negative 7 meters, and semiconductor physicists measure electron mass at 9.1 times 10 to the negative 31 kilograms. Negative exponents represent small, precision scales, not negative debt. Today, we will master the elevator rule, the power of a power rule, and tackle the grand master multi-variable simplifications on workbook page 11. Let's begin on slide 2!",
    "2": "Prof. Park: On slide 2, we introduce the Negative Exponent Property: a^(-n) = 1 / (a^n), and conversely 1 / (a^(-n)) = a^n, where the base a is non-zero. Sora, explain the elevator ticket rule in detail for our students.\nTA Sora: Think of a fraction as a two-story building: the penthouse upstairs is the numerator, and the ground floor downstairs is the denominator. Whenever a factor has a negative exponent, it is unhappy with its current floor! Its negative sign is literally a ticket that says: 'Move me to the other floor!' If an expression like x^(-3) is upstairs in the numerator, you move it downstairs to the denominator, and upon arrival, the ticket is punched—the negative exponent turns into a positive 3: 1 / (x^3).\nProf. Park: And what happens if the negative exponent is already downstairs in the denominator, like 1 / (y^(-4))?\nTA Sora: Then it takes the elevator upstairs to the numerator! It becomes y^(+4) in the penthouse, and its denominator becomes 1. Notice two crucial golden rules: First, moving a factor across the fraction bar changes ONLY the sign of the exponent, NEVER the sign of the base! If you have (-5)^(-3), the negative 5 stays negative 5—it simply moves downstairs as 1 / [ (-5)^3 ]. Second, only factors with negative exponents move. Factors with positive exponents stay right where they are. Let's practice with Example 4 on slide 3!",
    "3": "Prof. Park: Let's examine Section 1.2 Example 4 Parts A, B, and C on workbook page 10: Part A: x^(-4). Part B: 3^(-2). Part C: (-5)^(-2).\nTA Sora: In Part A, x^(-4) is in the numerator. We send x downstairs to the denominator, and the exponent becomes positive 4: 1 / (x^4). Simple, clean, and done!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part B: 3^(-2). A common student mistake is to multiply 3 by -2 and write -6. That is completely wrong! The negative sign belongs to the exponent, so it sends the base 3 downstairs: 1 / (3^2). Now 3 squared is 9, so our answer is 1/9! Notice that 1/9 is a positive fraction—the negative exponent never made the number negative.\nTA Sora: Now compare Part B with Part C: (-5)^(-2). Here, the base is -5 inside parentheses. The negative exponent -2 sends the entire base (-5) downstairs: 1 / [ (-5)^2 ]. Downstairs, (-5)^2 is (-5)(-5) = +25! So our final answer is 1/25.\nProf. Park: Sora, what happens if someone asks about 0^(-2)? Can zero have a negative exponent?\nTA Sora: That is a brilliant question! If you try 0^(-2), the elevator rule sends zero downstairs: 1 / (0^2) = 1/0! And from Lecture 02, we know dividing by zero is strictly UNDEFINED! That is why our exponent property explicitly specifies that the base a can NEVER be zero. For all non-zero bases, the elevator rule works flawlessly and predictably every single time.",
    "4": "Prof. Park: Now let's examine Example 4 Parts D, E, and F on slide 4: Part D: -5^(-2). Part E: 1 / (x^(-6)). Part F: (2/3)^(-3).\nTA Sora: Look at Part D: -5^(-2) without parentheses! Remember our base identification rule from Lecture 02: the exponent -2 applies ONLY to the number 5, NOT the negative sign out front! So the negative sign stays out front as -1. The 5 moves downstairs: - [ 1 / (5^2) ] = -1/25. Compare Part C: (-5)^(-2) = +1/25, whereas Part D: -5^(-2) = -1/25. That distinction is guaranteed to appear on your exam!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part E: 1 / (x^(-6)). Here, x^(-6) is downstairs in the denominator. It takes the elevator upstairs to the numerator, and the exponent becomes positive 6: x^6 / 1 = x^6!\nTA Sora: And look at Part F: (2/3)^(-3). A fraction raised to a negative power! When an entire fraction is raised to a negative exponent, the negative power flips the entire fraction to its reciprocal! (2/3)^(-3) becomes (3/2)^(+3). Now cube both top and bottom: 3^3 / 2^3 = 27/8! Flipping the fraction uses up the negative sign instantly. What a brilliant shortcut! Think of it as turning an hourglass upside down: the sand that was on top is now on the bottom, and the timer starts fresh. Whenever an entire rational fraction is wrapped in parentheses with a negative power, flip first, then distribute.",
    "5": "Prof. Park: Slide 5 introduces the Power Rules: 1. Power of a Power: (a^m)^n = a^(m · n). 2. Power of a Product: (ab)^n = a^n · b^n. 3. Power of a Quotient: (a/b)^n = a^n / b^n.\nTA Sora: Let's explain why (a^m)^n means multiplying exponents! Take (a^2)^3. What does that mean? It means taking three copies of a^2: (a^2) · (a^2) · (a^2). By the product rule from Lecture 05, we add: 2 + 2 + 2 = 6, which is 2 times 3! So when a power is raised to another power, you MULTIPLY the exponents: a^(2 · 3) = a^6.\nProf. Park: And when you have multiple factors inside parentheses raised to an outside power, like (2x^3)^4, the outside exponent distributes to EVERY single factor inside—including the numerical coefficient! So (2x^3)^4 = 2^4 · (x^3)^4 = 16 x^(3 · 4) = 16x^12.\nTA Sora: Never forget the coefficient! Students often write 2x^12, forgetting to raise 2 to the 4th power. Every citizen inside the parentheses gets hit by the outside exponent. Think of the outside exponent as rain falling on an open picnic table—everything on the table gets wet! Even in scientific notation, when you square (3 · 10^4)^2, you square 3 to get 9, and multiply exponents 4 · 2 = 8, giving 9 · 10^8. Treating every factor inside with equal respect guarantees no term is left unscaled.",
    "6": "Prof. Park: Let's look at Example 5 Parts A and B on slide 6: Part A: (y^4)^5. Part B: (-2a^3)^4.\nTA Sora: In Part A, (y^4)^5 is a textbook power of a power. We multiply the inside exponent by the outside exponent: 4 · 5 = 20. So our result is y^20. Done in one clean step!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part B: (-2a^3)^4. Here, the negative 2 is inside the parentheses, and the outside exponent is 4. We distribute the exponent 4 to both parts: First, (-2)^4. Since the exponent 4 is even, (-2)(-2)(-2)(-2) = +16. Second, (a^3)^4 = a^(3 · 4) = a^12. Combine them: +16a^12.\nTA Sora: Notice that an even power turns a negative coefficient positive: (-2)^4 = +16. If the outside power had been 3 (an odd power), (-2)^3 would have remained negative 8: -8a^9. And what if there were no parentheses: -2(a^3)^4? Then the 4 would only apply to a^3, giving -2a^12! Even powers absorb negatives inside parentheses; odd powers preserve them. Keep that rule in mind for rapid mental checking!\nProf. Park: In Bozeman's precision photonics sector, optical engineers calculate laser pulse frequencies where wavelengths scale as powers of 10 to the negative 6 meters. If an engineer forgets to distribute an outer negative power across numerical coefficients or drops an elevator sign, an entire optical sensor calibration is ruined!\nTA Sora: Exactly, Professor! That is why I always tell students: treat exponents like bank transaction entries. Every negative exponent is a pending wire transfer across the fraction bar line. Once it crosses over, the fee is paid and the sign becomes positive!",
    "7": "Prof. Park: Moving to slide 7 with Example 5 Parts C and D: Part C: (a^9 b^6)^5. Part D: (w^3 · w^8) / (w^5)^2.\nTA Sora: In Part C, (a^9 b^6)^5 has two variable factors inside. Distribute the outside exponent 5 to both: For a: (a^9)^5 = a^(9 · 5) = a^45. For b: (b^6)^5 = b^(6 · 5) = b^30. So our simplified term is a^45 b^30!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part D: (w^3 · w^8) / (w^5)^2. Here we combine the product rule, the power of a power rule, and the quotient rule! Let's simplify top and bottom separately: In the numerator: w^3 · w^8 = w^(3 + 8) = w^11. In the denominator: (w^5)^2 = w^(5 · 2) = w^10.\nTA Sora: Now we have w^11 / w^10. Apply the quotient rule: 11 - 10 = 1. So w^11 / w^10 = w^1 = w! Look how an expression with four exponents collapsed down to a single letter w. That is mathematical elegance in action. If someone tested this with w = 2: numerator would be 2^3 · 2^8 = 8 · 256 = 2048. Denominator: (2^5)^2 = 32^2 = 1024. 2048 divided by 1024 is exactly 2! The algebra works flawlessly with actual numbers.\nTA Sora: Here is an extra pro-tip for slide 7: whenever you have a fraction with multiple variables and coefficients raised to a negative power outside, you can also flip the ENTIRE fraction upside down right at the start to turn the outer exponent positive!\nProf. Park: That is the Fraction Inversion Property: (A/B)^(-n) = (B/A)^n! In our problem, flipping the fraction first gives [ z^(-4) / (3 x^(-2) y) ]^3, which leads to the exact same final answer: x^6 / (27 y^3 z^12). Having two independent ways to solve a problem is the greatest confidence builder in algebra!",
    "8": "Prof. Park: Now let's tackle Example 5 Part E on slide 8: Simplify fully: [ (2 x^6 w^5) / (3 x w^5) ]^3.\nTA Sora: When you have a fraction inside parentheses raised to an outside power, you have two choices: Choice 1: Distribute the power 3 to every single factor on top and bottom first. Choice 2: SIMPLIFY INSIDE FIRST, then raise to the power 3! Sora's Golden Rule: ALWAYS simplify inside the parentheses first! It shrinks your numbers before you raise them to powers.\nProf. Park: Let's follow Sora's advice and simplify inside first: Look at the w terms: w^5 on top and w^5 on the bottom. w^5 / w^5 = 1! They cancel out completely! Look at the x terms: x^6 on top and x^1 on the bottom. By the quotient rule: 6 - 1 = 5, so x^5 remains on top. The coefficients 2 and 3 cannot be reduced. So inside the parentheses, we are left with: (2x^5 / 3)!\nTA Sora: Look how much smaller that is! Now apply the outside cube 3 to everything: Numerator: 2^3 · (x^5)^3 = 8 x^(5 · 3) = 8x^15. Denominator: 3^3 = 27. Our final answer is: (8x^15) / 27. If we had distributed the cube first, we would have had 2^3 = 8, x^18, w^15 over 27 x^3 w^15, and then canceled. Simplifying inside first was much cleaner and saved us two full steps!",
    "9": "Prof. Park: Now we arrive at the ultimate master problem on slide 9: Example 5 Part F on workbook page 11: Simplify fully: [ (3 x^11 y^5) / (2 x^(-1) y^8) ]^(-2).\nTA Sora: This is the grand championship problem of Section 1.2! It has coefficients, positive exponents, negative exponents inside, and an outside negative exponent. Let's attack it methodically using our three-step protocol: Step 1: Simplify inside the parentheses first!\nProf. Park: Let's do that! Look at x: we have x^11 on top and x^(-1) on the bottom. The x^(-1) on the bottom takes the elevator upstairs to the numerator and becomes x^(+1)! Upstairs, we already have x^11, so x^11 · x^1 = x^(11 + 1) = x^12. Now look at y: y^5 on top and y^8 on the bottom. 8 - 5 = 3, so y^3 stays on the bottom. So inside our parentheses, we now have: (3 x^12) / (2 y^3).\nTA Sora: Step 2: Look at the outside exponent: it is -2! The negative sign on the outside exponent flips the entire inside fraction to its reciprocal: [ (2 y^3) / (3 x^12) ]^(+2)! Step 3: Now square every factor inside: Numerator: 2^2 · (y^3)^2 = 4 y^(3 · 2) = 4 y^6. Denominator: 3^2 · (x^12)^2 = 9 x^(12 · 2) = 9 x^24. Assemble the final fraction: (4 y^6) / (9 x^24)! Every exponent is positive, coefficients 4 and 9 share no common factors, and each variable appears once. Perfection!",
    "10": "Prof. Park: What a triumph! On slide 10, we synthesize all 5 examples from Section 1.2 into our Master Summary Table. Sora, let's review the final golden rules.\nTA Sora: 1. Zero Exponent: a^0 = 1 (for non-zero base). 2. Product Rule: multiplying identical bases -> ADD exponents. 3. Quotient Rule: dividing identical bases -> SUBTRACT exponents. 4. Negative Exponents: the elevator rule—move across the fraction bar to turn positive. 5. Power of a Power: multiply exponents. 6. Power of a Fraction: simplify inside first, flip if the outside power is negative, then distribute to every factor.\nProf. Park: Mastering these exponent laws is essential because they form the operational foundation for polynomial multiplication in Section 1.3, FOIL in Section 1.4, rational expressions in Section 1.5, and all factoring techniques in Unit 2. In Lecture 07, we advance to Section 1.3 on workbook page 12: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials. Outstanding work today, everyone, and we will see you in Lecture 07!\nProf. Park: Let us also review the difference between (-x)^2 and -x^2 one last time before we leave exponents:\nTA Sora: In (-3)^2, the negative is inside parentheses, so (-3)(-3) = +9. In -3^2, the negative is OUTSIDE, so -(3)(3) = -9! Keep this distinction on a sticky note on your computer screen!"
}

SCRIPTS_L07 = {
    "1": "Prof. Park: Welcome everyone to Lecture 07 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me at the desk is TA Sora. Today, we open Section 1.3 of our course workbook, found on page 12: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials.\nTA Sora: Hello everyone! Today we transition from working with single-term monomials to working with polynomials—expressions that contain multiple terms combined by addition and subtraction. In the real world, polynomials are the mathematical models behind everything from cost and revenue curves in business, to trajectory curves of aircraft landing at Bozeman Yellowstone International Airport, to stress loads on construction framing in Gallatin Valley residential builds. For example, a business in Belgrade might model its total revenue as R(x) = 150x - 0.5x^2, and its production costs as C(x) = 3000 + 40x. To find profit, they subtract the cost polynomial from the revenue polynomial: P(x) = R(x) - C(x)! Understanding how to classify, add, subtract, and distribute polynomials is the foundational engine of business, engineering, and data science.\nProf. Park: Let's review the essential vocabulary on slide 1: A monomial has exactly 1 term (like 5x^3). A binomial has exactly 2 terms (like 2x + 7). A trinomial has exactly 3 terms (like 4x^2 - 5x + 9). And any algebraic expression composed of one or more terms is a polynomial. Each term consists of a numerical coefficient and variable factors. The degree of a single term is the sum of the exponents on its variables: for instance, 4x^3 has degree 3, while 7x^2 y^4 has degree 2 + 4 = 6! The degree of an entire polynomial is the highest degree of any term present. Sora, what is the golden requirement for combining terms in a polynomial?\nTA Sora: LIKE TERMS ONLY! Two terms are 'like terms' if and only if they have the exact same variable raised to the exact same exponent! For example, 5x^2 and 3x^2 are like terms because both have x squared. But 5x^2 and 5x are NOT like terms because one has power 2 and the other has power 1. When you combine like terms, you add or subtract the numerical coefficients, and the variable part remains completely unchanged. Let's practice on slide 2!",
    "2": "Prof. Park: Let's examine Section 1.3 Examples 1A and 1B on workbook page 12: Part A: Simplify 6y + 12 - 7y. Part B: Simplify -3x^2 + 5x + 9x^2 - 2.\nTA Sora: In Part A, we have three terms: 6y, +12, and -7y. Let's identify the like terms: 6y and -7y both have the variable y to the first power. The constant 12 is on its own. Combine the like terms by adding their numerical coefficients: 6 - 7 = -1. So 6y - 7y = -1y, which in algebra we write simply as -y. Now bring along the constant +12: our simplified expression is -y + 12 (or 12 - y). Notice how the variable y didn't change at all—it did not become y squared, and it did not disappear. Only the count of how many y's we have changed from 6 and -7 to -1.\nProf. Park: Now look at Part B: -3x^2 + 5x + 9x^2 - 2. Here we have terms of degree 2, degree 1, and degree 0 (the constant). Let's group the degree 2 terms: -3x^2 and +9x^2. Combine their coefficients: -3 + 9 = +6, so we have 6x^2. Now look for other degree 1 terms: we have +5x, but no other x terms to combine with, so it stays +5x. And the constant -2 is alone, so it stays -2. Assembling in descending order of exponents gives: 6x^2 + 5x - 2.\nTA Sora: Why do we write it in descending order? Writing polynomials in descending degree order—highest power first, down to the constant—is standard mathematical etiquette. It makes it easy to identify the leading coefficient, which is 6, the degree of the polynomial, which is 2 (a quadratic trinomial), and ensures everyone grading your paper can immediately confirm your answer. You can also align them in vertical columns on your paper: place degree 2 terms in one column, degree 1 in the next, and constants at the end, just like multi-digit addition in grade school!",
    "3": "Prof. Park: Moving to slide 3, let's tackle Examples 1C and 1D: Part C: 3n^2 - 17n - n^2 + 4n. Part D: 4ab^2 + 6a^2 b - 8ab^2 + 2a^2 b.\nTA Sora: In Part C, notice the term -n^2. Whenever a variable appears with a negative sign but no written number in front, its invisible coefficient is 1! So -n^2 is really -1n^2. Always write that little '1' with your pencil if you are prone to skipping it. Now let's group the n^2 terms: 3n^2 - 1n^2 = (3 - 1)n^2 = 2n^2. Next, group the linear n terms: -17n + 4n = (-17 + 4)n = -13n. Combine them: 2n^2 - 13n. Students often ask: 'Can I combine 2n^2 - 13n into -11n?' Absolutely not! n^2 and n are completely different dimensions—one is an area of size n by n, and the other is a length of size n. They cannot be combined!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part D—this is a classic exam trap: 4ab^2 + 6a^2 b - 8ab^2 + 2a^2 b. Sora, both terms contain a and b, so why aren't all four of them like terms?\nTA Sora: Because the exponents on the individual variables are different! In 4ab^2, the b is squared: a^1 b^2. In 6a^2 b, the a is squared: a^2 b^1. Think of construction materials: ab^2 is a board of width a and length b squared, while a^2 b is a board of width a squared and length b! They have different geometric shapes and cannot be stacked together. Let's combine matching categories: For the ab^2 group: 4ab^2 - 8ab^2 = (4 - 8)ab^2 = -4ab^2. For the a^2 b group: 6a^2 b + 2a^2 b = (6 + 2)a^2 b = +8a^2 b. Our final answer is: -4ab^2 + 8a^2 b (or 8a^2 b - 4ab^2). Always match the exponent to the specific letter!",
    "4": "Prof. Park: Slide 4 takes us to Example 2 Parts A and B on page 12: Adding and Subtracting Polynomial Groups: Part A: (5x^3 + 2x^2 - 13x) + (4x^3 - 8x^2 + 2x). Part B: (7x^2 - 5x + 3) - (2x^2 - 9x - 6).\nTA Sora: In Part A, we are ADDING two polynomial groups. When there is an addition sign (+) between the parentheses, the parentheses serve only as containers and can simply be removed without altering any signs! Let's remove the parentheses and group like terms: Cubic terms: 5x^3 + 4x^3 = 9x^3. Quadratic terms: 2x^2 - 8x^2 = -6x^2. Linear terms: -13x + 2x = -11x. Our resulting polynomial is: 9x^3 - 6x^2 - 11x.\nProf. Park: But in Part B, we have SUBTRACTION: (7x^2 - 5x + 3) - (2x^2 - 9x - 6). Sora, what is the single biggest trap on algebra exams with polynomial subtraction?\nTA Sora: Students only subtract the very first term, 2x^2, and leave the other terms untouched! That minus sign sitting outside the second parentheses acts as a multiplier of negative 1: -1 · (2x^2 - 9x - 6). It MUST distribute to EVERY single term inside that second set of parentheses! Watch what happens to the signs: - (2x^2) becomes -2x^2. - (-9x) becomes +9x! The negative times negative becomes positive! - (-6) becomes +6! The negative times negative becomes positive!\nProf. Park: Rewrite the entire line without parentheses: 7x^2 - 5x + 3 - 2x^2 + 9x + 6. Now combine like terms: 7x^2 - 2x^2 = 5x^2. -5x + 9x = +4x. +3 + 6 = +9. Our final simplified result is: 5x^2 + 4x + 9! Distributing that negative sign across all three terms is the secret to 100% accuracy on subtraction problems. If you have time, you can even check your answer by adding the result (5x^2 + 4x + 9) back to the subtracted group (2x^2 - 9x - 6) to verify it yields the original (7x^2 - 5x + 3)!",
    "5": "Prof. Park: Now let's explore the Distributive Property with Monomials on slide 5: Examples 3A and 3B on workbook page 13: Part A: -2x(3x^2 + 8x - 4). Part B: 4x^2(2x^3 - 5x + 7).\nTA Sora: The Distributive Property says: a(b + c + d) = ab + ac + ad. The monomial sitting outside multiplies every single term inside the parentheses! Think of a physical rectangular field in Gallatin County partitioned into three sections: the common width is -2x, and the three lengths are 3x^2, 8x, and -4. The total area is the sum of the three individual section areas! And remember: when multiplying variable terms, we use the product rule from Lecture 05—we MULTIPLY the coefficients and we ADD the exponents!\nProf. Park: Let's distribute -2x to each of the three terms in Part A: First term: (-2x)(3x^2). Multiply coefficients: -2 · 3 = -6. Add exponents: x^1 · x^2 = x^3. Result: -6x^3. Second term: (-2x)(8x). Multiply coefficients: -2 · 8 = -16. Add exponents: x^1 · x^1 = x^2. Result: -16x^2. Third term: (-2x)(-4). Negative times negative is positive! (-2)(-4) = +8, with x attached: +8x.\nTA Sora: Assemble the three products: -6x^3 - 16x^2 + 8x. Notice the signs flipped completely: positive 3x^2 became negative, positive 8x became negative, and negative 4 became positive! Now let's do Part B: 4x^2(2x^3 - 5x + 7). Term 1: (4x^2)(2x^3) = (4 · 2) x^(2 + 3) = 8x^5. Term 2: (4x^2)(-5x^1) = (4 · -5) x^(2 + 1) = -20x^3. Term 3: (4x^2)(7) = (4 · 7) x^2 = +28x^2. Combine them: 8x^5 - 20x^3 + 28x^2. Notice that there are no like terms among x^5, x^3, and x^2, so this polynomial is fully simplified.\nProf. Park: Think of residential construction framing across Bozeman and Belgrade: when contractors calculate structural perimeter lumber or roof truss dimensions with varying spans, variables represent modular lumber dimensions. Missing terms just mean zero length in that specific component—they do not vanish or break the formula!\nTA Sora: That is why I tell students to write in placeholder zeros if it helps them stay aligned on paper: write + 0x^3 as a placeholder column. That way, degree 4, 3, 2, 1, and 0 terms line up in neat vertical columns like an accounting ledger!",
    "6": "Prof. Park: Moving to slide 6, let's examine Examples 3C and 3D: Part C: -x(x^2 - 4) - 2(x^3 + 2x - 5). Part D: 3x^2(x - 4) + 2x(x^2 - 5x + 1).\nTA Sora: In Part C, we have TWO separate distributive blocks separated by subtraction: Block 1: -x(x^2 - 4). Distribute -x: (-x)(x^2) = -x^3, and (-x)(-4) = +4x. Block 2: -2(x^3 + 2x - 5). Distribute -2: (-2)(x^3) = -2x^3, (-2)(2x) = -4x, and (-2)(-5) = +10.\nProf. Park: Now write all terms in a single line and combine like terms: -x^3 + 4x - 2x^3 - 4x + 10. Cubic terms: -1x^3 - 2x^3 = -3x^3. Linear terms: +4x - 4x = 0x! They cancel each other out completely! Constant: +10. So our simplified result is simply: -3x^3 + 10!\nTA Sora: Now let's conquer Part D: 3x^2(x - 4) + 2x(x^2 - 5x + 1). Distribute the first block: 3x^2(x) - 3x^2(4) = 3x^3 - 12x^2. Distribute the second block: 2x(x^2) - 2x(5x) + 2x(1) = 2x^3 - 10x^2 + 2x. Now combine like terms: Cubic terms: 3x^3 + 2x^3 = 5x^3. Quadratic terms: -12x^2 - 10x^2 = -22x^2. Linear term: +2x. Final polynomial: 5x^3 - 22x^2 + 2x. Modularity makes even long problems simple.\nProf. Park: Multi-variable polynomials appear constantly in physics, such as calculating kinetic energy or heat dissipation across rectangular panels where length x and width y both vary simultaneously.\nTA Sora: And notice the order in each term: by mathematical convention, we write variable factors in alphabetical order: x before y, a before b. This makes spotting like terms instantaneous!",
    "7": "Prof. Park: To conclude Lecture 07, let's look at slide 7: Nested Grouping Symbols—Examples 3E and 3F: Part E: 3 + 2[ 5x - 4(x - 9) ] + 7. Sora, when grouping symbols are nested inside brackets, how do we proceed?\nTA Sora: Work strictly from the INSIDE OUT! Always locate the innermost parentheses first. Here, the innermost part is -4(x - 9). Distribute -4: (-4)(x) = -4x, and (-4)(-9) = +36. Now look inside the brackets: [ 5x - 4x + 36 ]. Combine like terms inside the brackets: 5x - 4x = x. So the brackets collapse to [ x + 36 ]!\nProf. Park: Look how clean that is! Now our expression is: 3 + 2[ x + 36 ] + 7. Next, distribute the outside 2 into the brackets: 2(x) = 2x, and 2(36) = 72. Now our full line is: 3 + 2x + 72 + 7. Now simply combine all the constants: 3 + 72 = 75, plus 7 = 82! So our final simplified answer is: 2x + 82.\nTA Sora: Look at how an intimidating problem with brackets, parentheses, and four operations collapsed into a simple two-term binomial: 2x + 82! When students try to rush and distribute from the outside in, they create enormous fractions and sign errors. Working from the inside out guarantees clarity at every step. In Lecture 08, we advance to Section 1.4: Multiplying Polynomials and the FOIL Method on workbook page 14. Practice combining like terms tonight, and we will see you in Lecture 08!\nProf. Park: Let us emphasize one final time the difference between adding terms and multiplying terms:\nTA Sora: When adding like terms: 3x^2 + 5x^2 = 8x^2. The coefficients add, but the exponent NEVER changes! When multiplying: (3x^2)(5x^2) = 15x^4. The coefficients multiply and the exponents ADD! Confusing these two rules is the number one cause of lost points on Exam 1!"
}

SCRIPTS_L08 = {
    "1": "Prof. Park: Welcome everyone to Lecture 08 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me is TA Sora. Today, we explore Section 1.4 of our course workbook, spanning pages 14 and 15: Multiplying Polynomials and the FOIL Strategy.\nTA Sora: Hello everyone! Today we learn how to multiply binomials and polynomials together. This is one of the most famous and heavily used procedures in all of secondary and college mathematics. Whether you are finding the area of a newly planned building footprint in Bozeman, calculating economic revenue functions (price times quantity) for a business in Belgrade, or factoring quadratic equations in Unit 2, polynomial multiplication is the universal engine behind it all.\nProf. Park: That is right, Sora. When we multiply two binomials—like (a + b)(c + d)—every term in the first binomial must multiply every term in the second binomial. To help students remember all four multiplications without missing any, educators developed the famous acronym: FOIL! F stands for First terms. O stands for Outer terms. I stands for Inner terms. L stands for Last terms.\nTA Sora: You can also think of FOIL geometrically as an area model or a 2-by-2 Punnett square: draw a rectangle partitioned into 4 smaller cells. The top sides are a and b, and the left sides are c and d. The areas of the four cells are ac, ad, bc, and bd! When you sum the cells together, you get the exact same result. Visual learners in architectural drafting and carpentry often love the box model, while algebraic thinkers love the rhythmic flow of FOIL. Let's examine how the FOIL strategy works on slide 2 with Example 1!\nTA Sora: Imagine you are designing a high-performance energy-efficient modular home for a client building in the Bridger Foothills. If one room module has length (2x + 5) feet and width (3x - 4) feet, calculating the total square footage requires finding the product of those two binomials.\nProf. Park: Every square foot of flooring, insulation, and solar radiant heating depends on correctly multiplying these algebraic dimensions! That is why polynomial multiplication is not an abstract puzzle—it is physical reality in construction and architecture.",
    "2": "Prof. Park: Let's examine Section 1.4 Examples 1A and 1B on workbook page 14: Part A: (2x + 4)(3x + 5). Part B: (x - 6)(x + 8).\nTA Sora: Let's execute FOIL step by step on Part A: (2x + 4)(3x + 5): F - First terms: multiply the first terms of each binomial: (2x)(3x) = 6x^2. O - Outer terms: multiply the terms on the far outside: (2x)(5) = +10x. I - Inner terms: multiply the terms on the inside: (4)(3x) = +12x. L - Last terms: multiply the last terms of each binomial: (4)(5) = +20.\nProf. Park: Now write all four products in a line: 6x^2 + 10x + 12x + 20. Notice that the two middle terms—the Outer and Inner terms—are like terms! Both have x to the first power. 10x + 12x = 22x. Combining them gives our final trinomial: 6x^2 + 22x + 20!\nTA Sora: Now let's tackle Part B: (x - 6)(x + 8). Watch your signs carefully here! F - First: (x)(x) = x^2. O - Outer: (x)(8) = +8x. I - Inner: (-6)(x) = -6x. L - Last: (-6)(8) = -48. Combine the middle like terms: +8x - 6x = +2x. Our final trinomial is: x^2 + 2x - 48. Notice the predictable rhythm: First gives the quadratic term, Outer and Inner combine to give the linear term, and Last gives the constant. If you do this 5 times, your hand will automatically glide through FOIL without even pausing to think.\nProf. Park: You can also visualize monomial multiplication as distributing paint across a series of connected rectangular rooms.\nTA Sora: That is a wonderful visual! 3x^2 is the height of the wall, and (4x^3 - 5x + 7) is the broken-up length of three adjoining rooms. The total area is the sum of the three individual room areas: 12x^5 - 15x^3 + 21x^2!",
    "3": "Prof. Park: Now look at slide 3 with Examples 1C and 1D: Binomial Products with Leading Constants: Part C: 2(b - 3)(b + 4). Part D: -3(2y - 1)(3y + 4).\nTA Sora: In Part C, we have three factors: the constant 2, and two binomials (b - 3) and (b + 4). Students often ask: 'Should I distribute the 2 first, or FOIL the binomials first?' And more dangerously, some students distribute the 2 to BOTH binomials—writing (2b - 6)(2b + 8)! That is completely wrong—that would be multiplying by 4, not 2! Multiplication is associative, so 2 multiplies only once.\nProf. Park: That is such a vital distinction, Sora. Sora's Golden Tip is: FOIL the binomials first, keep them inside protective parentheses, and distribute the leading constant at the very end! Let's follow that advice for Part C: FOIL (b - 3)(b + 4): First: b · b = b^2. Outer: b · 4 = +4b. Inner: -3 · b = -3b. Last: -3 · 4 = -12. Combine middle terms: 4b - 3b = +b. So the binomial product is (b^2 + b - 12). Now multiply the entire trinomial by the leading 2: 2(b^2 + b - 12) = 2b^2 + 2b - 24!\nTA Sora: Look how effortless that was! Now look at Part D: -3(2y - 1)(3y + 4). FOIL the binomials first: First: (2y)(3y) = 6y^2. Outer: (2y)(4) = +8y. Inner: (-1)(3y) = -3y. Last: (-1)(4) = -4. Combine middle terms: 8y - 3y = +5y, giving (6y^2 + 5y - 4). Now distribute the leading -3: (-3)(6y^2) = -18y^2. (-3)(+5y) = -15y. (-3)(-4) = +12. Final result: -18y^2 - 15y + 12. Methodical execution produces effortless accuracy.\nTA Sora: Here is a memory hook for FOIL: First, Outer, Inner, Last. Notice that Outer and Inner are the 'middle children' of the problem—they almost always share the exact same variable power and combine together into a single middle term!\nProf. Park: But always be cautious with your signs: if the outer product is negative and the inner product is positive, you must add integers carefully: -10x + 12x = +2x.",
    "4": "Prof. Park: Slide 4 highlights the single most notorious trap in introductory algebra: Squaring a Binomial! Part E: (2y - 7)^2. Part F: 2(y - 7)^2. Sora, sound the alarm!\nTA Sora: MAXIMUM ALERT LEVEL! (2y - 7)^2 does NOT equal (2y)^2 - 7^2! Students who distribute the exponent 2 get 4y^2 - 49 or 4y^2 + 49, and they lose all points on the problem! Exponents do NOT distribute across addition or subtraction! Think geometrically: if you have a square of side (a + b), its area is (a + b)^2. That large square contains a square of size a^2, a square of size b^2, AND two large rectangles of size a · b! If you write a^2 + b^2, you threw away both rectangles! That is like buying a house and forgetting the living room!\nProf. Park: That is a wonderful mental visual! Squaring a binomial means multiplying that entire binomial by ITSELF! Whenever you see (2y - 7)^2, immediately write it as two side-by-side parentheses: (2y - 7)(2y - 7)! Now FOIL: First: (2y)(2y) = 4y^2. Outer: (2y)(-7) = -14y. Inner: (-7)(2y) = -14y. Last: (-7)(-7) = +49. Now look at the middle terms: -14y and -14y do NOT cancel! They ADD: -14y - 14y = -28y! So (2y - 7)^2 = 4y^2 - 28y + 49. The middle term -28y is essential!\nTA Sora: And look at Part F: 2(y - 7)^2. First write (y - 7)^2 as (y - 7)(y - 7). FOIL gives: y^2 - 7y - 7y + 49 = y^2 - 14y + 49. Now distribute the leading 2: 2(y^2 - 14y + 49) = 2y^2 - 28y + 98. Write out both binomials every time, and you will never drop the middle term.\nProf. Park: Let us draw the geometric area model for (3x + 4)^2: draw a large square of side length 3x + 4.\nTA Sora: Split each side into two segments: 3x and 4. That divides the large square into FOUR distinct rooms!\nOne room is 3x by 3x = 9x^2.\nOne room is 4 by 4 = 16.\nAnd there are TWO identical hallway rooms, each measuring 3x by 4 = 12x!\nThe two hallway rooms together give 12x + 12x = 24x!\nIf you forget the middle term, you are literally erasing two whole rooms from your house blueprint!",
    "5": "Prof. Park: Let's expand our horizons on slide 5 with Examples 1H and 1I: Multiplying Binomials by Trinomials: Part H: (w + 2)(w^2 - 2w + 9). Part I: (2x - 3)(x^2 + 4x - 5).\nTA Sora: When multiplying a 2-term binomial by a 3-term trinomial, FOIL expands into the generalized distributive property: Each of the 2 terms in the first group must multiply each of the 3 terms in the second group. 2 times 3 equals 6 total multiplications!\nProf. Park: Let's do Part H systematically: First, distribute w to all three terms: w(w^2) = w^3. w(-2w) = -2w^2. w(9) = +9w. Next, distribute +2 to all three terms: 2(w^2) = +2w^2. 2(-2w) = -4w. 2(9) = +18.\nTA Sora: Now align like terms: w^3 is alone. Quadratic terms: -2w^2 + 2w^2 = 0w^2! They cancel completely! Linear terms: +9w - 4w = +5w. Constant: +18. Our final result is: w^3 + 5w + 18!\nProf. Park: Now let's tackle Part I: (2x - 3)(x^2 + 4x - 5). Distribute 2x to all three terms: (2x)(x^2) = 2x^3. (2x)(4x) = +8x^2. (2x)(-5) = -10x. Now distribute -3 to all three terms: (-3)(x^2) = -3x^2. (-3)(4x) = -12x. (-3)(-5) = +15.\nTA Sora: Now combine like terms: Cubic: 2x^3. Quadratic: 8x^2 - 3x^2 = +5x^2. Linear: -10x - 12x = -22x. Constant: +15. Final polynomial: 2x^3 + 5x^2 - 22x + 15! Six multiplications combined into four clean terms.\nProf. Park: Notice why difference of squares is so unique: because the two middle terms are exact opposites (+35x and -35x), they cancel each other out completely!\nTA Sora: It is like paying 35 dollars and immediately receiving 35 dollars back—your net change is zero! That leaves only the square of the first term minus the square of the second term: 25x^2 - 49. Whenever you see conjugate binomials (A - B)(A + B), you can instantly write down A^2 - B^2!",
    "6": "Prof. Park: Moving to slide 6, let's examine Examples 2 and 3: Real-World Geometry Applications on workbook page 15. A rectangle has length L = 3x - 4 and width W = 2x + 1. Example 2 asks for the Perimeter. Example 3 asks for the Area.\nTA Sora: This is where algebra directly connects to physical space and cost estimating! In Gallatin County construction, if a general contractor is installing baseboard trim around the room, they need the Perimeter. If they are ordering hardwood flooring planks, they need the Area! For Perimeter, the distance all the way around a rectangle is: P = 2L + 2W. Substitute our algebraic expressions: P = 2(3x - 4) + 2(2x + 1). Distribute: 2(3x) - 2(4) = 6x - 8. And 2(2x) + 2(1) = 4x + 2. Combine like terms: 6x + 4x = 10x, and -8 + 2 = -6. So Perimeter P = 10x - 6 linear units!\nProf. Park: Now Example 3 asks for the Area. The area of a rectangle is length times width: A = L · W. So we must multiply the two binomials: A = (3x - 4)(2x + 1)! This is a job for FOIL: First: (3x)(2x) = 6x^2. Outer: (3x)(1) = +3x. Inner: (-4)(2x) = -8x. Last: (-4)(1) = -4. Combine middle terms: 3x - 8x = -5x. So Area A = 6x^2 - 5x - 4 square units!\nTA Sora: Notice the clear distinction: Perimeter is an addition problem of degree 1 (10x - 6), while Area is a multiplication problem of degree 2 (6x^2 - 5x - 4). The mathematical degree matches the physical dimensions!\nTA Sora: When multiplying a binomial by a trinomial, I strongly recommend writing your work in a 2-by-3 grid or lining up like terms vertically:\nRow 1 from distributing 2x: 2x^3 + 8x^2 - 10x.\nRow 2 from distributing -3:       - 3x^2 - 12x + 15.\nLining up the x^2 and x terms vertically makes combining them practically foolproof!\nProf. Park: Vertical alignment prevents students from skipping terms or accidentally combining unlike powers. Neatness on paper translates directly into 100% test scores!",
    "7": "Prof. Park: To conclude Lecture 08, let's examine Example 4 on slide 7: Modified Rectangle Dimensions on workbook page 15. A given rectangle has length 2x + 3 and width x - 6. If the length is increased by 4 and the width is decreased by 2, find the simplified polynomial expression for the new area.\nTA Sora: Let's adjust the dimensions first: Original Length = 2x + 3. Increased by 4: (2x + 3) + 4 = 2x + 7. Original Width = x - 6. Decreased by 2: (x - 6) - 2 = x - 8. Now our new dimensions are Length = 2x + 7 and Width = x - 8!\nProf. Park: Now find the new area by multiplying the modified binomials: Area = (2x + 7)(x - 8). Execute FOIL: First: (2x)(x) = 2x^2. Outer: (2x)(-8) = -16x. Inner: (7)(x) = +7x. Last: (7)(-8) = -56. Combine the middle like terms: -16x + 7x = -9x. Our new area is: 2x^2 - 9x - 56 square units!\nTA Sora: Let's do a quick reality check: suppose x = 10 feet. Original dimensions would be 2(10)+3 = 23 ft by 10-6 = 4 ft, area = 92 sq ft. New dimensions: 2(10)+7 = 27 ft by 10-8 = 2 ft, area = 54 sq ft. Now test our algebraic formula: 2(10)^2 - 9(10) - 56 = 2(100) - 90 - 56 = 200 - 146 = 54 sq ft! It matches with 100% precision. In Lecture 09, we transition to Section 1.5 on workbook page 16: Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions. We will apply our fraction arithmetic from Lecture 01 to algebraic fractions with variables in the denominators. Fantastic work today, everyone, and we will see you in Lecture 09!\nTA Sora: And here is a secret preview: in Chapter 2, we will do this exact process in REVERSE! Reversing multiplication is called FACTORING!\nProf. Park: If you master FOIL and distribution today, factoring next week will feel completely natural and intuitive. Excellent work mastering polynomial multiplication!"
}
